<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Foix</h1><p class="st-meta">1012 CE – 1607 CE (approx.) · capital Foix</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pyrenean county that sheltered Cathars, produced the hunter-prince Gaston Fébus and passed through Navarre to Henry IV.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1012 the lands of the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a> were divided, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-roger-of-foix">Bernard-Roger</a> received the region around Foix, which became a separate county under the castle above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariege-river">Ariège</a>. His son Roger I is the first to be called count of Foix.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a mountainous land of pasture and iron mines. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-roger-of-foix">Raymond-Roger</a> fought the crusaders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, and many of his family supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>. In 1290 the counts inherited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Béarn</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iii-of-foix">Gaston III Fébus</a> ruled both from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthez">Orthez</a> as a near-sovereign prince and wrote a famous hunting book.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The house of Foix inherited the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> in 1479, then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albrets</a>. Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Jeanne d'Albret</a> it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a>, who in 1607 united his personal lands, including Foix, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-foix.jpg" alt="County of Foix" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_County_of_Foix.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1012 CE – 1607 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Foix</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Raymond-Roger of Foix, Gaston III Fébus, Henry IV of France</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Foix</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=county-of-foix" aria-label="County of Foix on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4719 652 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Foix lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5039 908L5002 900M5039 908L5054 728M5039 908L4991 897M5039 908L4960 913M5039 908L5037 922"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5002 900h0M5054 728h0M4991 897h0M4960 913h0M5037 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5039 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5039 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Foix. <a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=county-of-foix">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
